> Goodman and Prine originally composed the song as a pastiche and style parody of "every country song" they had ever heard. In live performances, Goodman would often adopt a parody of Hank Williams Sr.''s performance style, with a large cowboy hat. Waylon Jennings, Charley Pride and Merle Haggard (as well as his song "The Fightin'' Side of Me") are mentioned in the lyrics; Coe also uses loose impersonations of each artist in doing so, and also makes reference to Faron Young''s "Hello Walls" in the background vocals, noting that "you don''t have to call me" any of those names anymore. In the third verse, Coe notes "the only time I know I''ll hear David Allan Coe is when Jesus has his final Judgment Day."', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You_Never_Even_Called_Me_by_My_Name', 'David Allan Coe - 16 Biggest Hits', 'Waylon Jennings, Charley Pride, Merle Haggard, Faron Young', null, true);
